                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 812,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 1216,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Measures how spread out the values of an array are: the standard deviation, the typical distance of a value from the mean.\n\nValues packed tightly together give a small std; values scattered widely give a large one; values all the same give 0. It is in the same units as the data, so a std of 0.2 on readings in metres means they typically sit about 20 cm either side of their average. Reach for it to tell a steady signal from a jittery one, to measure noise, or to set a threshold in \"how many std from the mean\".\n\nHOW IT IS WORKED OUT:\nTake the mean; take each value's distance from it; square those distances and average them (that average is the variance, np.var); take the square root to get back to the data's units. The average divides by the number of values N - the \"population\" std. Some tools (a spreadsheet's STDEV, for one) divide by N - 1 instead, which gives a slightly larger answer for small arrays: for 1 2 3 4 np.std gives 1.118, STDEV 1.291.\n\nTHE axis MENU:\nWith no axis (the default) the whole array is reduced to one number. With axis 0 the reduction runs DOWN each column, giving one result per column; with axis 1 it runs ALONG each row, giving one result per row. For a table of 5 frames (rows) of 4 sensors (columns), axis 0 answers \"what did each sensor read on average\", axis 1 \"what did all the sensors read on average at each frame\". The axis named is the one that disappears: a 5 by 4 array gives 4 values on axis 0, 5 on axis 1.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.std <axis: int>\nWith no argument there is no axis: one std for the whole array.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.std\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list or a single number is turned into one). Every array that arrives is measured and the result sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\naxis (menu, also an inlet):\nWhich way to reduce - see THE axis MENU. Changing it does NOT send by itself; the next array uses it. The menu offers None (the whole array) and the axes the last array had (None and 0 for a 1-D array); a message None into the axis inlet also returns to the whole array. An axis the array does not have is treated as its last axis for that array only: the choice is kept, so one 1-D array through np.std 1 does not stop the 2-D arrays that follow from being averaged along axis 1.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nstd:\nWith no axis, one number, never negative. With an axis, an array one dimension smaller: one std per column (axis 0) or per row (axis 1).\n\nTHE DEMO:\nmake_noise makes 64 random values, scaled so that at spread 1 and shift 0 their mean is exactly 0 and their standard deviation exactly 1. The spread slider multiplies them all; the shift slider adds to them all. The plot shows the 64 values as points.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nDrag spread: the cloud of points widens and np.std follows it exactly - spread 2, std 2. Drag shift: the whole cloud moves up or down, np.mean follows it, and np.std does not change at all. The mean says WHERE the values are, the std how SPREAD they are; the two are independent. At spread 0 every value is the same and the std is 0.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.var is the std squared - same information, squared units. np.mean is the centre the std is measured from. np.robust_mean uses a sturdier measure of spread (the median distance from the median) to reject outliers. np.amax and np.amin give the extremes rather than the typical spread.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
